# The ledger of `sql`: what does not depend on the database, measured against
# Go's `database/sql` and `database/sql/driver` (Go 1.27, `go doc -all` on
# both, 2026-11-16) and Python's DB-API 2.0 (PEP 249, which is a document
# and no module: its names as the PEP writes them, with `sqlite3`'s on this
# machine, Python 3.15, where it adds to them). Rust's standard library has
# no database, so its line is `-` in every block. What a connection does,
# opening, running, preparing and transactions, stands in the ledger of
# `sql/postgres`, which writes the API every driver keeps. Expected values of
# the tests were observed from Python's `sqlite3` on 2026-11-16.

capability sql/value  a value as it travels to a database and back
  heroes  sql.Value, sql.kind_of
  go      any, driver.Value, driver.IsValue, driver.IsScanValue
  rust    -
  python  None, int, float, str, bytes, datetime.datetime, STRING, BINARY, NUMBER, DATETIME, ROWID
  note    a variant of seven cases: null, boolean, integer, float, text, blob, instant. Go's driver.Value has the same six plus nil; PEP 249's type objects become `kind_of`'s names. A date and a timestamp are an `instant`; a time of day alone has no case and travels as text

capability sql/null  a NULL told apart from every value
  heroes  sql.Value, sql.is_null_at, sql.is_null_named
  go      NullBool, NullByte, NullFloat64, NullInt16, NullInt32, NullInt64, NullString, NullTime, Null, Null.Scan, Null.Value, NullBool.Scan, NullBool.Value, NullByte.Scan, NullByte.Value, NullFloat64.Scan, NullFloat64.Value, NullInt16.Scan, NullInt16.Value, NullInt32.Scan, NullInt32.Value, NullInt64.Scan, NullInt64.Value, NullString.Scan, NullString.Value, NullTime.Scan, NullTime.Value, driver.Null, driver.NotNull
  rust    -
  python  None
  note    one case, `null_value`, in place of Go's nine wrappers: a `match` on a value answers it, and a typed read of a NULL fails `invalid` as Go's Scan into an int64 does

capability sql/parameters  values bound to a statement's placeholders, never spliced into its text
  heroes  sql.Value
  go      DB.Exec args, driver.NamedValue, driver.Valuer, driver.ValueConverter, driver.ColumnConverter, driver.DefaultParameterConverter, driver.NamedValueChecker, driver.Bool, driver.Int32, driver.String
  rust    -
  python  Cursor.execute parameters, paramstyle
  note    a `[sql.Value]` beside the statement's text, in the order of its placeholders, which are the database's own (`$1` for PostgreSQL, `?1` for SQLite). Go converts any value through Valuer; here a program builds the case it means

capability sql/named-parameters  parameters given by name
  heroes  refused: one way to bind a value, a list by position; PostgreSQL's protocol has no named parameters, and a program that names them keeps its own order
  go      Named, NamedArg
  rust    -
  python  paramstyle "named", "pyformat"

capability sql/out-parameters  a stored procedure's output parameters
  heroes  refused: neither PostgreSQL's protocol nor SQLite has them; a procedure's `INOUT` values come back as a row of `query`
  go      Out
  rust    -
  python  Cursor.callproc

capability sql/row  a row of a query's answer, its values and its columns' names
  heroes  sql.Row, sql.Rows
  go      Row, Rows, driver.Rows
  rust    -
  python  the tuple Cursor.fetchone answers, sqlite3.Row
  note    every row of a query is read before it answers, into `sql.Rows`; reading one row at a time is the ledger of `sql/postgres`'s

capability sql/read-by-place  a column's value read by its place in the row
  heroes  sql.value_at, sql.boolean_at, sql.integer_at, sql.float_at, sql.text_at, sql.blob_at, sql.instant_at
  go      Row.Scan, Rows.Scan, ConvertAssign, Scanner, RawBytes, driver.ScanContext, driver.RowsColumnScanner
  rust    -
  python  row[i]
  note    a typed read converts nothing: an integer is not read as a float nor a text as an integer, where Go's Scan converts between them (a string to an int64). A place past the row fails `not_found`, a value of another kind `invalid`

capability sql/read-by-name  a column's value read by its name
  heroes  sql.value_named, sql.boolean_named, sql.integer_named, sql.float_named, sql.text_named, sql.blob_named, sql.instant_named
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  sqlite3.Row[name], sqlite3.Row.keys
  note    names compared byte for byte, the first of two of one name answering, as observed of `sqlite3.Row`; but `sqlite3.Row` ignores ASCII case (`row["id"]` read a column `Id`), which PostgreSQL's quoted names forbid, so here `id` is not `Id`

capability sql/columns  the names and types of a query's columns
  heroes  sql.Rows, sql.Column
  go      Rows.Columns, Rows.ColumnTypes, ColumnType, ColumnType.Name, ColumnType.DatabaseTypeName, driver.RowsColumnTypeDatabaseTypeName
  rust    -
  python  Cursor.description (its name and type_code)
  note    `type_name` is the database's own spelling of the type, as Go's DatabaseTypeName is

capability sql/column-details  a column's length, precision and scale, whether it may be NULL, the type a program reads it as
  heroes  pending
  go      ColumnType.Length, ColumnType.DecimalSize, ColumnType.Nullable, ColumnType.ScanType, driver.RowsColumnTypeLength, driver.RowsColumnTypePrecisionScale, driver.RowsColumnTypeNullable, driver.RowsColumnTypeScanType
  rust    -
  python  Cursor.description (display_size, internal_size, precision, scale, null_ok)
  note    PostgreSQL sends a type modifier from which length, precision and scale are read, and whether a column may be NULL needs a question to its catalogue; not written in this wave

capability sql/result  how many rows a statement changed
  heroes  sql.Result
  go      Result, Result.RowsAffected, driver.Result, driver.RowsAffected, driver.ResultNoRows
  rust    -
  python  Cursor.rowcount
  note    0 for a statement that counts none, where PEP 249 says -1: a count is never a sign

capability sql/last-id  the id of the last row inserted
  heroes  sql.Result
  go      Result.LastInsertId
  rust    -
  python  Cursor.lastrowid
  note    `last_id` is an `i64?` failing `unsupported` where the database keeps none, as PostgreSQL does (Go's lib/pq answers an error, Python's psycopg None)

capability sql/failure-codes  the codes a database failure is told with
  heroes  sql.INVALID, sql.NOT_FOUND, sql.EXISTS, sql.PERMISSION, sql.DOES_NOT_FIT, sql.CLOSED, sql.TIMEOUT, sql.STOPPED, sql.LIMIT, sql.UNSUPPORTED, sql.FAILED
  go      ErrNoRows, ErrConnDone, ErrTxDone, driver.ErrBadConn, driver.ErrSkip, driver.ErrRemoveArgument
  rust    -
  python  Warning, Error, InterfaceError, DatabaseError, DataError, OperationalError, IntegrityError, InternalError, ProgrammingError, NotSupportedError
  note    README § A4's codes, no others: PEP 249's IntegrityError splits into `exists` (a unique key) and `invalid` (another constraint), DataError into `does_not_fit` and `invalid`, OperationalError into `closed`, `timeout`, `limit` and `failed`, NotSupportedError is `unsupported`; Go's ErrNoRows is `not_found`, ErrConnDone `closed`, ErrTxDone `invalid`. Warning is no failure: a driver keeps it beside the answer

capability sql/database-code  the database's own code and words kept in a failure
  heroes  sql.failure_message, sql.database_code_of
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  sqlite3.Error.sqlite_errorcode, sqlite3.Error.sqlite_errorname
  note    a failure's message opens with the database's code in brackets, `[23505] ...`, since a `T?` carries a code and a message only; `database_code_of` reads it back. Go's standard library leaves it to each driver's error type

capability sql/quote-identifier  a name quoted for a statement's text
  heroes  sql.quote_identifier, sql.quote_qualified
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    standard SQL's double quotes, each inner quote doubled, observed by Python's `sqlite3` reading every quoted name back as itself; an empty name is refused although SQLite accepts one, since PostgreSQL refuses a zero-length name, and a zero byte is refused by both. Go's lib/pq has QuoteIdentifier and psycopg `sql.Identifier`, outside the standard libraries

capability sql/quote-literal  a value quoted into a statement's text
  heroes  refused: a value travels as a parameter and never in the text, the package's rule; a statement that takes no parameters names no value of the program's
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -

capability sql/time-values  a date or a moment made as a value
  heroes  sql.Value, time.date, time.from_unix
  go      time.Time in an any
  rust    -
  python  Date, Time, Timestamp, DateFromTicks, TimeFromTicks, TimestampFromTicks
  note    an `instant` holds a `time.Instant`, which `time` makes from a calendar date or from seconds since 1970; PEP 249's Time, a time of day alone, has no case and is written as text

capability sql/binary  bytes made as a value
  heroes  sql.Value
  go      []byte in an any
  rust    -
  python  Binary
  note    the `blob` case

capability sql/drivers  a driver chosen by its name at run time
  heroes  refused: a driver is a package the program names with `use`; the language has no interface for a registry to hold
  go      Register, Drivers, Open, OpenDB, DB.Driver, driver.Driver, driver.DriverContext, driver.Connector
  rust    -
  python  connect

dropped
  go      driver.Conn, driver.ConnBeginTx, driver.ConnPrepareContext, driver.Execer, driver.ExecerContext, driver.Pinger, driver.Queryer, driver.QueryerContext, driver.SessionResetter, driver.Validator, driver.Stmt, driver.StmtExecContext, driver.StmtQueryContext, driver.Tx, driver.TxOptions, driver.IsolationLevel, driver.RowsNextResultSet: the interfaces a driver implements for `database/sql` to call; here every driver is called directly and keeps the API written in `sql/postgres`
  go      DBStats, DB.Stats, DB.SetConnMaxIdleTime, DB.SetConnMaxLifetime, DB.SetMaxIdleConns, DB.SetMaxOpenConns, DB.Conn, Conn.Raw: the pool of connections `database/sql` keeps behind a DB; a Rebel connection is one connection, and a pool is a program's own
  python  apilevel, threadsafety: the PEP's module attributes, which describe a module to a generic caller; a connection here belongs to the thread that holds its place
